  {
    "episode_id": 49226,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49226.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "And that's a great question, Josh, because I don't think conservative has to equal crazy. And I remember, you know, I came in in a very bizarre way, probably more unique than anybody in Congress and that the individual before me, my predecessor, he resigned because of alcoholism. I don't know if you remember that or not, Josh. So I only had five days to get ready for a an abnormal committee vote. And I only won by one vote. I mean, that wasn't a crushing mandate in the nominating contest. But to do that, I had to make my first political decision. And that's when I knew that this was something that I had found distasteful, is that they said, hey, Denver, listen, if you don't join the Freedom Caucus, you can't win the vote. And I'm like, ah, that's BS, come on. They're like, well, join it. Now, I know people might find this crazy, but I had not really been involved in politics at all. All my work was behind the door and intelligence. So I really thought the Freedom Caucus was going to be sort of a like-minded group of constitutional scholars that wanted to debate. And, you know, what could be the big deal to me join the Freedom Caucus? So we put out a statement, said, Hey, I'll join the Freedom Caucus, and that might have put me over on that one vote, I do not know. But that was my first, I would say real political decision, and one that really was interesting because I went to about three or four meetings total, I think, and I was good, right? I sort of moved on from that.",
