  {
    "episode_id": 49226,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49226.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I mean, you sort of have to play the game. I'm pretty blunt about this, Josh. You probably know, you know, but the thing is, is that when I gave my speech, everybody was really raw, raw Trump. I went over the four specific policy issues I thought we should do. And so, you know, and that was my speech at the whatever you call it, the committee convention that we did. And so what I would always say is I can agree with Trump's policies, but I don't have to agree with his behavior. That's sort of how I went down the line. And being quiet, you know, I remember you know, comms and consultants would say, Denver, you know, I know you want to get out there, but let's just see if we can get through this. Let's see if we can be quiet because Trump is the, is the most powerful portion of the Republican Party. And so I tried to, and there is obviously policies I agreed with, you know, I was massive when it came to the USMCA. There are some things I just thought this is, this is good policy. But it got to a point, I would say about a year into my term that that just the erratic behavior and some of the things that you start to see and some of the information that's being pushed, I thought could be damaging. But again, I was screaming into a popcorn box, Josh. It wasn't a lot of people listening to me at that point.",
