  {
    "episode_id": 49226,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49226.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, yeah. And I think the thing is though, is that when you look at the local polling, Josh, and you look at the fundraising numbers, and you look at the committee chairs, which can control your fate at the grassroots level, depending on the state, right? I mean, if you have open primaries or jungle primaries, it's a little bit different. But for places like me in Virginia, where you have no choice, right? They can vote a convention of 12 people to kick you out, which has nothing to do with the electorate. They can disenfranchise as many people as they want to control the party. You have to fall in line. And that means you almost have to have a tongue that's split in two. You got to lick Trump's boots and you got to lick the committee's boots. And I refuse to do either. And that, again, people said I was either arrogant or cocky. But these were the people that were arrogant and cocky who wanted to get paid that lacked any real talent except fixing conventions and extorting politicians, which seems to be the MO for a lot of the people here in Virginia for sure. But I would say it's the same way across the country in a lot of these districts.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "In Virginia, committee chairs can unilaterally remove a candidate through a convention of 12 people.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker refuses to conform to demands from both Trump supporters and party committee chairs.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views conformity with powerful party figures as requiring disingenuous behavior akin to 'licking boots'.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
