    "turn_text": "I think you're making some extremely important points about just how dramatic and affect social media in particular has had in shaping people's perceptions of the news, of reality, of public policy. Can you expound on that a little bit? I mean, is this more? I mean, the QAnon phenomenon, I was sort of caught sleeping on it and January 6 was just a just a stomach turning event that you would never have imagined happening in this country. But how worried are you that the toothpaste can't get put back in the tube? And you also see, you know, right and left alike, you see these sort of crazy ideas percolating among You know, small but powerful communities online. There was a story that Ben Smith of the New York Times wrote this weekend about jeopardy contestants believing this this insane conspiracy theory that one of the contestants who was a victor was winning what was, you know, I guess showing off secret white supremacist memes or hand signals, and it turned out it was total nonsense. But these ideas spread like wildfire and any of the people who spread them can get awfully influential online. How do you combat this and how important is this in terms of fixing our future politics?",
