    "turn_text": "it's a big issue and you know i mentioned that this morning uh... on the uh... incredible platform twitter uh... but uh... about this uh... you know the jeopardy contestant who is accused of flashing you know, racist, you know, hand signals, which was obviously absurd, right? All he had to do was look at that it was his third win, right? One, two, three. And the fact that somebody would make hay out of that and former jeopardy contestants are supposed to have an IQ above moron is something I've been trying to say for some time is that I've had multiple people come through my place of business that are, you know, professionals. doctors, lawyers, bankers, people that have postgraduate degrees, doctorates that still believe in some things that are easily disprovable. And a lot of that has to do with the fact that there's confirmation bias, but they're also in that swim lane of wanting to belong to the tribe. And the tribe whips that up over and over again and whips up this type of information bias over and over again. So when you see what's happening, like with the Jeopardy contestant, you see this weird It's almost like this EKG that's very constant of conspiracy theory identifiers that just sort of morph based on the information and based on confirmation bias. And again, I want to tell people that this isn't education specific. I mean, you might have more ignorant people think that, honestly, that there's a democratic of all blood sucking Satanists, right? I mean, that's That's possible. But think about something a little bit more, you know, intellectual, that there's this secret, you know, ability of influencers able to identify individuals almost directly before time to flash coded language and signals over public spaces to actually force some kind of action that quickly. And, you know, that's very difficult to do. And that is also conspiracy thinking. And I always say, Josh, you know, the far right might be in a hold my beer moment, but crazy is 