    "turn_text": "right? And the other issue that we have too is that that money has to come from somewhere. And if you do overheat, as you're looking at inflation right now, looking at some of the other things that are happening, I was just stunned they were talking about raising the corporate tax rate and raising the capital gains tax rate after an economic hardship that so many Americans went through. It's short-sighted. I don't know who's in his ear, but I think economically it's going to crash. You can't continue to spend money this way without some way of actually mitigating what those harmful effects are going to be. And my guess is when you just saw today the Biden administration using COVID money, $2 billion and putting it down to the border. Now I'm not going to get into the border politics right now. What I'm saying is we do have money in reserve that we need to use initially. And I think the $1.9 or $2 trillion infrastructure package is excessive. There's a lot of things in there that have nothing to do with infrastructure. I still think the $800 billion Republican plan is probably going to be a little better. And I guess it's going to be about $1.2 trillion. But I guess what I'm saying, Josh, is that there has to come a time where there's fiscal responsibility and where stimulus is no longer needed. And I think a lot of that has to do with buying votes. And that's easy to do nowadays. And the right does it too. But right now, I think the left is saying that they have this very fast mandate. I think they're looking at polling, they're looking at redistricting and saying that 2022 is not looking good for the Dems and the outside. And they're trying to push a lot of stuff through. I think that's going to backfire. Again, if you had individuals there that hadn't been in politics their whole life and maybe had some business acumen, they would say, we need to pull back on the throttle here. moderate our stances and try to do what's best for america without throwing money at every problem and trying to keep out any social justice on everything because it gets to the point. The people working for a living room wait a second i'm suffering just like everybody else not everything is racism not everything is a social justice issue some things are just to go to work issue so i think that's the balance right josh is that. Yeah, people like me out here that actually run companies that have run companies, very successful companies saying, hey, we're going too far with this. And on the flip side of this, and here's where I really want to just shock people, I would think that the stimulus for our distillery has created an amazing revenue stream. Um, could it go away? It might not, but it's, it's given us amazing labor issues. So that's the thing. What happens when government gets too involved is you start getting cascading effects on both sides that are very difficult, very difficult to control. And right now I think the government is way too involved in our economy. And that's why again, I'm in a, in a bizarre place as a fiscal conservative and more of a social libertarian.",
